<h1>Telescopic &amp; Extendable Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A telescopic conveyor extends and retracts to reach from a loading dock deep into truck trailers, shipping containers, and railcars. Nested conveyor sections slide inside one another, extending the conveying surface to match the trailer depth. <a href="https://caljan.com/technology/caljan-classic/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Caljan states its Classic telescopic conveyor</a> can cut truck loading and unloading times in half compared with manual handling. The belt does the carrying, so workers stay at the stacking face instead of walking the trailer length with each case, package, or bag.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Telescopic Conveyor Types</h2>
<h3>Telescopic Belt Conveyors</h3>
<p>Telescopic belt conveyors use a continuous belt that extends with the conveyor frame. The belt loops through all nested sections, with belt take-up mechanisms compensating for length changes as the conveyor extends and retracts. Belt versions handle the widest range of product types: cartons, poly bags, mailer packages, irregularly shaped items, and products that would jam between rollers.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/telescopic-caljan-performer-dock.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="Red and black telescopic belt conveyors side by side at dock positions inside a parcel building, with lights on the front of the nearest unit." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Caljan Performer telescopic conveyors at a customer site, shown retracted at the dock. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://caljan.com/technology/caljan-performer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Caljan</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Telescopic Roller Conveyors</h3>
<p>Telescopic roller conveyors use nested sections of gravity or powered rollers. As the conveyor extends, roller sections slide out to cover the additional length. Gravity versions rely on a slight incline for product flow. Powered versions use motorized rollers or belt-under-roller drives for positive product transport. Roller telescopic conveyors suit uniform, flat-bottom products like corrugated cases and plastic totes. They cost less than belt versions but cannot handle bags, polybags, or irregularly shaped products.</p>
<h3>Boom Conveyors (Extendable Belt Loaders)</h3>
<p>Boom conveyors are simpler extendable belt conveyors that raise and lower the discharge end (boom) to stack products at different heights within the trailer. They extend horizontally and the boom end articulates vertically, allowing workers to load or unload at waist height throughout the trailer. Boom conveyors are common at parcel carrier hubs for loading and unloading package trailers.</p>
<h3>Mobile Telescopic Conveyors</h3>
<p>Mobile telescopic conveyors mount on casters or wheels and move between dock doors as needed. A facility with many dock doors but only a few active at one time can run a small fleet of mobile units that move to whichever doors are active, instead of installing a fixed conveyor at every door. Mobile units connect to dock-level power outlets. Confirm the supply voltage and phase with the manufacturer before planning dock electrical work.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Telescopic Conveyors Work</h2>
<h3>Extension Mechanism</h3>
<p>Nested conveyor sections slide on linear bearings or roller guide rails. An electric or hydraulic drive powers the extension and retraction. Each added section increases reach relative to the retracted length. Caljan offers its Classic in multiple section configurations reaching up to 16 meters (about 52 feet) and markets a 5-section Performer model for longer reach. The operator advances or retracts the boom in steps as the trailer fills or empties.</p>
<h3>Loading Operation</h3>
<p>For truck loading, the telescopic conveyor starts fully extended, with the discharge end at the trailer nose. Workers or automated systems place products on the dock-end infeed. Products travel the belt to the trailer end where a worker stacks them. As the trailer fills from nose to tail, the operator retracts the conveyor progressively to keep the discharge point near the stacking face. This eliminates the need for workers to carry products deep into the trailer.</p>
<h3>Unloading Operation</h3>
<p>For truck unloading, the conveyor starts retracted. Workers at the trailer nose place products onto the belt, which transports them back to the dock. As the trailer empties from tail to nose, the operator extends the conveyor deeper into the trailer. This eliminates the long carry distance from trailer nose to dock that causes worker fatigue and injury in manual unloading operations.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/telescopic-caljan-droop-snoot-unload.webp" width="768" height="512" alt="Two workers in high-visibility vests take cartons from a stacked wall inside a trailer, with the belt of a telescopic conveyor carrying a carton behind them." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Two operators unload a trailer onto a Caljan telescopic conveyor fitted with Caljan's Droop Snoot. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://caljan.com/technology/caljan-performer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Caljan</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Height Adjustment</h3>
<p>Most telescopic conveyors include height adjustment at the dock end and/or trailer end. Hydraulic or electric actuators raise or lower the conveyor surface to match the trailer floor height, which varies by trailer type (dry van, reefer, container chassis). Caljan states the Classic can elevate the whole telescopic conveyor up to 10 degrees to set operator working height. Some units also tilt the conveyor to maintain a slight decline toward the dock during unloading for gravity assist.</p>
<h2 id="benefits-heading">Productivity Benefits</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Manual Handling</th>
<th>With Telescopic Conveyor</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Carry distance</td>
<td>Workers walk the trailer length with each case</td>
<td>Belt carries product between dock and stacking face</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Loading and unloading time</td>
<td>Set by walking and carrying</td>
<td>Set by stacking rate. Caljan claims up to half the manual time</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Staffing</td>
<td>Extra people to carry</td>
<td>Stacker at the face, feeder at the dock</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product damage</td>
<td>Throwing and dropping during long carries</td>
<td>Less handling between dock and trailer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ergonomic risk</td>
<td>High (lifting, carrying)</td>
<td>Lower (belt does the carrying, lifting remains)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Measure your own baseline (minutes per trailer, people per door) before and after installation. Results depend on product weight, trailer configuration, and workforce. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Telescopic Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Distribution center outbound shipping:</strong> Telescopic conveyors load filled cases from <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a> into trailers at shipping docks. Cases arrive on a conveyor trunk line from the pack-out area, transfer onto the telescopic conveyor, and travel into the trailer where a worker stacks them. This is the most common telescopic conveyor application.</p>
<p><strong>Parcel carrier hubs:</strong> UPS, FedEx, and USPS facilities use boom and telescopic conveyors at every inbound and outbound dock door. Parcel hubs run high, continuous package volumes. Telescopic conveyors handle the high-volume, mixed-product stream of packages, polybags, and envelopes that flow through sort centers.</p>
<p><strong>Manufacturing inbound receiving:</strong> Telescopic conveyors unload incoming raw materials, components, and packaging supplies from delivery trucks. Workers at the trailer nose place cases onto the belt, and the conveyor transports them to the dock for palletizing or direct transport to production. Inbound receiving is often overlooked for automation but offers significant labor savings.</p>
<p><strong>Container unloading:</strong> Shipping containers (20' and 40') unload at port facilities, intermodal yards, and destination warehouses. Telescopic conveyors extend into the container through the doors. <a href="https://www.dsv.com/en/our-solutions/modes-of-transport/sea-freight/shipping-container-dimensions/dry-container" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DSV lists the interior width of standard 20 ft and 40 ft dry containers</a> at 2.35 m (about 7.7 ft), so the conveyor boom plus the operator must fit in that width. Some units mount on tracks alongside the container for precise positioning.</p>
<p><strong>Postal and e-commerce fulfillment:</strong> E-commerce fulfillment centers use telescopic conveyors to load mixed-size parcels into delivery trailers. The variety of package sizes (small envelopes to large boxes) favors belt telescopic conveyors over roller versions because belts handle all sizes without jamming.</p>
<p><strong>Agricultural and food loading:</strong> Telescopic conveyors load bags of grain, feed, fertilizer, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food products</a> into trailers. Heavy bagged products cause fatigue and injury when carried by hand into trailers. The <a href="https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/110725" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Revised NIOSH Lifting Equation</a> treats horizontal reach, travel distance, and lift frequency as risk factors. The telescopic conveyor removes the carry, and workers place bags on the belt at the trailer end.</p>
<h2 id="spec-heading">Specifying Telescopic Conveyors</h2>
<h3>Extension Range</h3>
<p>Match the extension range to the longest trailer type the dock handles. Measure from the dock edge to the nose of the longest trailer you handle, and add any dock leveler length the boom must cross. DSV lists interior lengths of 5.9 m (19.4 ft) for a 20 ft dry container and 12.03 m (39.5 ft) for a 40 ft dry container. Over-specifying extension adds cost and weight without benefit. Under-specifying forces workers to carry products for the remaining distance.</p>
<h3>Belt Width</h3>
<p>Belt width must accommodate the largest product while fitting inside the trailer. <a href="https://caljan.com/technology/caljan-performer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Caljan lists Performer belt widths</a> of 600, 800, and 1000 mm (about 24, 31, and 39 in.) and the Classic at 600 and 800 mm. Narrow belts fit container interiors. Wide belts handle large cases and reduce edge spill. Caljan rates Performer belt speed as variable from 0.2 to 0.8 m/s and the Classic from 0.3 to 0.7 m/s. Set speed to what the stacker can keep up with.</p>
<h3>Fixed vs. Mobile</h3>
<p>Fixed installations bolt to the dock floor at a dedicated door. They suit high-volume doors that load/unload continuously throughout the shift. Mobile units roll between doors on casters. They suit facilities with intermittent dock activity across many doors. Mobile units need setup time at each door to position and connect power, but serve multiple doors with a single investment.</p>
<h3>Integration with Dock Systems</h3>
<p>Telescopic conveyors integrate with dock levelers, dock shelters, truck restraints, and facility conveyor systems. The conveyor infeed connects to the facility's takeaway conveyor or direct from <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor</a> trunk lines. Controls interface with warehouse management systems (WMS) for load tracking and door assignment. Safety interlocks prevent conveyor operation while dock doors are closed or truck restraints are disengaged. For docks that also use forklifts, <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.178" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.178(m)(7)</a> requires brakes set and wheel blocks in place to prevent trailer movement during loading and unloading.</p>
